WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.625 --> 00:00:02.794
(host)
A primordial mother,
a divine female who births

00:00:02.877 --> 00:00:07.257
the first generation of deities
in Mesopotamian mythology.

00:00:07.340 --> 00:00:10.343
Tiamat is crucial
to life and death.

00:00:10.427 --> 00:00:12.887
Tiamat makes up
the salty waters of creation.

00:00:12.971 --> 00:00:16.349
The ocean is known for
its prolific capacity

00:00:16.433 --> 00:00:18.101
to generate living organisms.

00:00:18.184 --> 00:00:20.228
Life is born from the sea.

00:00:20.311 --> 00:00:22.856
In the case of Tiamat,
so are monsters.

00:00:22.939 --> 00:00:25.525
This ancient Mesopotamian
goddess produced

00:00:25.608 --> 00:00:27.569
11 different frightening beasts,

00:00:27.652 --> 00:00:30.071
from dragons to scorpion men,

00:00:30.155 --> 00:00:32.866
but is Tiamat herself
really all that frightening?

00:00:32.949 --> 00:00:35.660
Is the mother of
monsters even a monster?

00:00:35.744 --> 00:00:37.662
[dramatic music]

00:00:37.746 --> 00:00:41.166
I'm Dr. Emily Zarka,
and this is "Monstrum."

00:00:41.249 --> 00:00:43.501
Much of Mesopotamian
mythology attributes

00:00:43.585 --> 00:00:46.546
natural phenomena to
intelligent deities.

00:00:46.629 --> 00:00:48.506
This becomes
particularly significant

00:00:48.590 --> 00:00:51.760
in relation to rainfall, since
the region's early adoption

00:00:51.843 --> 00:00:55.680
of an irrigation agricultural
system defined its success

00:00:55.764 --> 00:00:57.015
and helped secure
the development

00:00:57.098 --> 00:01:00.018
of numerous large-scale
permanent settlements.

00:01:00.101 --> 00:01:03.480
Due to soil conditions, land
could easily become flooded

00:01:03.563 --> 00:01:06.691
and salinized, making
cultivation challenging

00:01:06.775 --> 00:01:09.194
and presumably at
the whim of the gods.

00:01:09.277 --> 00:01:11.738
So it makes sense that
one of their central myths

00:01:11.821 --> 00:01:14.282
would involve two
primordial bodies of water,

00:01:14.365 --> 00:01:17.285
Apsu, source of all
fresh water, and Tiamat,

00:01:17.368 --> 00:01:19.037
the source of all saltwater.

00:01:19.120 --> 00:01:20.705
Tiamat's written history begins

00:01:20.789 --> 00:01:23.750
around the second half of
the second millennium BCE

00:01:23.833 --> 00:01:26.252
in the Enuma Elis,
now commonly known

00:01:26.336 --> 00:01:27.879
as the Babylonian Epic
of Creation.

00:01:27.962 --> 00:01:31.049
These cuneiform tablets
are the backbone

00:01:31.132 --> 00:01:32.801
of Mesopotamian mythology.

00:01:32.884 --> 00:01:36.012
Her appearance in this
text is ambiguous.

00:01:36.095 --> 00:01:39.182
It reveals she is female,
but little else is known.

00:01:39.265 --> 00:01:41.810
Although she is at
least somewhat humanoid,

00:01:41.893 --> 00:01:44.729
it also indicates that
she is made of water.

00:01:44.813 --> 00:01:46.105
This story gets a bit confusing

00:01:46.189 --> 00:01:48.441
so I have this family
tree to help us out.

00:01:48.525 --> 00:01:50.360
The language in
the text suggests

00:01:50.443 --> 00:01:52.612
that the primordial
deities' mingling

00:01:52.695 --> 00:01:55.448
involved biological
reproduction with Apsu

00:01:55.532 --> 00:01:58.284
as begetter and father
of all and Tiamat

00:01:58.368 --> 00:02:01.621
as the mother who births
all from her womb.

00:02:01.704 --> 00:02:04.457
All of the original gods in
the Mesopotamian pantheon

00:02:04.541 --> 00:02:06.084
are produced by these two.

00:02:06.167 --> 00:02:08.169
As their progeny
in turn procreate,

00:02:08.253 --> 00:02:11.297
each god becomes more
powerful than the next.

00:02:11.381 --> 00:02:16.052
Ansar and Kisar's son Anu begot
Ea, also know as Nudimmud.

00:02:16.135 --> 00:02:18.471
Ea was the champion
among his fathers,

00:02:18.555 --> 00:02:21.558
god of wisdom, magic, and water.

00:02:21.641 --> 00:02:23.518
Known for his
trickery and mischief,

00:02:23.601 --> 00:02:26.729
he and the other Gods amused
themselves by making a racket

00:02:26.813 --> 00:02:29.315
within Tiamat's belly,
where they lived.

00:02:29.399 --> 00:02:32.652
One day, Ea overhears Apsu,
who we might consider

00:02:32.735 --> 00:02:35.530
his great-grandfather,
complaining to Tiamat

00:02:35.613 --> 00:02:39.033
about their children and
suggesting that they be killed.

00:02:39.117 --> 00:02:42.412
Horrified and angered,
Tiamat refuses,

00:02:42.495 --> 00:02:45.498
suggesting they take more
lenient disciplinary action,

00:02:45.582 --> 00:02:47.125
which Apsu ignores.

00:02:47.208 --> 00:02:49.878
Ea slaughters Apsu
and uses his body

00:02:49.961 --> 00:02:53.506
to create a new dwelling
place which is named Apsu,

00:02:53.590 --> 00:02:55.633
which is morbid AF.

00:02:55.717 --> 00:02:58.678
All of this is still in
Tiamat's womb, by the way.

00:02:58.761 --> 00:03:01.973
Patricide complete,
Ea has his own son Marduk,

00:03:02.056 --> 00:03:04.517
the strongest yet of the gods.

00:03:04.601 --> 00:03:07.395
Marduk is gifted with the
power of the four winds

00:03:07.478 --> 00:03:10.982
and uses them to stir up
a flood wave in Tiamat's womb,

00:03:11.065 --> 00:03:13.943
which exacerbates
all the other gods.

00:03:14.027 --> 00:03:17.405
In a colossal act of
retaliation, either motivated

00:03:17.488 --> 00:03:19.908
by revenge or insult
depending on interpretation,

00:03:19.991 --> 00:03:24.370
Tiamat births 11
monsters into the world.

00:03:24.454 --> 00:03:26.456
Girl made an army of kids,

00:03:26.539 --> 00:03:29.250
ones with sharp teeth
and poison for blood,

00:03:29.334 --> 00:03:31.920
giant serpents, dragons,
and raging dragons;

00:03:32.003 --> 00:03:35.131
apparently there
is a distinction,

00:03:35.214 --> 00:03:38.927
hairy hero men, lion men,
weather demons, snakes,

00:03:39.010 --> 00:03:41.846
scorpion men,
fish men, bull men,

00:03:41.930 --> 00:03:45.433
and the fantastically
ambiguous aggressive demons.

00:03:45.516 --> 00:03:49.395
A bit panicked by
this influx of creations,

00:03:49.479 --> 00:03:51.689
the other gods grant
Marduk the power

00:03:51.773 --> 00:03:53.816
to create and destroy.

00:03:53.900 --> 00:03:56.277
Ea further equips
his son with a scepter,

00:03:56.361 --> 00:03:58.905
throne, staff,
and other weaponry.

00:03:58.988 --> 00:04:01.491
Marduk makes a bow,
calls up some winds

00:04:01.574 --> 00:04:04.077
and thunderbolts,
and readies for battle.

00:04:04.160 --> 00:04:07.413
Marduk provokes Tiamat with
insults and she launches

00:04:07.497 --> 00:04:11.125
into a hasty attack,
leaving herself vulnerable.

00:04:11.209 --> 00:04:14.087
She is captured in a net
and overwhelmed by the winds

00:04:14.170 --> 00:04:18.591
before Marduk's arrow pierces
her stomach, tearing her open.

00:04:18.675 --> 00:04:21.386
He manages to subdue
her monstrous children

00:04:21.469 --> 00:04:23.846
before returning to
Tiamat, crushing her skull

00:04:23.930 --> 00:04:26.599
with a mace and slitting
her arteries open.

00:04:26.683 --> 00:04:30.061
Marduk uses one half of her
dead body to form the heavens

00:04:30.144 --> 00:04:32.271
and the other to
craft the earth.

00:04:32.355 --> 00:04:34.607
From her eyes,
he creates the Euphrates

00:04:34.691 --> 00:04:38.695
and Tigris Rivers, from her
breasts he makes mountains.

00:04:38.778 --> 00:04:41.155
What is left of her
becomes the oceans.

00:04:41.239 --> 00:04:44.659
He traps Tiamat's monstrous
offspring at his feet

00:04:44.742 --> 00:04:47.662
and forms images of them
to place at the entrance

00:04:47.745 --> 00:04:51.791
of the dwelling Apsu to remind
the world of their existence.

00:04:51.874 --> 00:04:54.377
The Tiamat story can be
better understood by looking

00:04:54.460 --> 00:04:57.422
at earlier, seemingly
disparate mythologies

00:04:57.505 --> 00:04:59.090
that all blended
together to create

00:04:59.173 --> 00:05:00.842
our monster mother's story.

00:05:00.925 --> 00:05:03.344
Remember how Tiamat
birthed 11 monsters

00:05:03.428 --> 00:05:05.054
to avenge her partner's death?

00:05:05.138 --> 00:05:09.392
Well, that number pops up in
other, older Mesopotamian lore

00:05:09.475 --> 00:05:13.229
like the Tale of Ninurta,
an ancient Sumerian warrior god

00:05:13.312 --> 00:05:17.233
associated with agriculture
who slew 11 enemies

00:05:17.316 --> 00:05:19.027
before designing the world.

00:05:19.110 --> 00:05:22.572
And two Sumerian texts from
the late third millennium BCE

00:05:22.655 --> 00:05:25.491
contain a list of monster
kin eerily similar

00:05:25.575 --> 00:05:27.577
to those attributed to Tiamat.

00:05:27.660 --> 00:05:28.995
And how many were there? 11.

00:05:29.078 --> 00:05:32.040
Then there are monstrous
deities associated

00:05:32.123 --> 00:05:35.084
with the tumultuous sea,
such as the Canaanite Yam.

00:05:35.168 --> 00:05:37.086
Gendered masculine,
he is described

00:05:37.170 --> 00:05:39.922
as both a serpent
and a dragon with seven heads,

00:05:40.006 --> 00:05:43.885
depending on the source,
able to wield chaotic power.

00:05:43.968 --> 00:05:47.764
Like Tiamat, Yam was defeated
by a heroic young god, Baal.

00:05:47.847 --> 00:05:50.725
Tiamat herself appears
by name in an evocation

00:05:50.808 --> 00:05:53.895
as patron of a fierce
warrior in a text

00:05:53.978 --> 00:05:57.607
from Central Mesopotamia
around 2350 BCE.

00:05:57.690 --> 00:06:00.818
Still nothing in the Canaanite
or Sumerian narratives

00:06:00.902 --> 00:06:04.781
suggest Tiamat would be
a monster, just a powerful god.

00:06:04.864 --> 00:06:07.325
Her great power is
admired and feared

00:06:07.408 --> 00:06:10.495
but with respectful reverence
more than outright horror,

00:06:10.578 --> 00:06:13.873
a potentially monstrous god
rather than strictly a monster.

00:06:13.956 --> 00:06:16.125
But what would make
something a monster

00:06:16.209 --> 00:06:18.586
by Sumerian or
Akkadian standards?

00:06:18.669 --> 00:06:20.797
Well, there was no singular word

00:06:20.880 --> 00:06:23.174
to categorize
fearsome creatures,

00:06:23.257 --> 00:06:24.217
but their monstrosity
was marked

00:06:24.300 --> 00:06:27.345
in images and texts
by a combination

00:06:27.428 --> 00:06:31.933
of predatory animal features
like teeth, wings, and claws,

00:06:32.016 --> 00:06:34.644
visual traits that would
be grafted onto Tiamat

00:06:34.727 --> 00:06:37.146
in the Middle Bronze Age
and first millennium.

00:06:37.230 --> 00:06:39.649
Marduk was once only
a local god said

00:06:39.732 --> 00:06:41.943
to preside over the
city of Babylon.

00:06:42.026 --> 00:06:45.446
His mythical status grew along
with the rise of Hammurabi,

00:06:45.530 --> 00:06:47.281
the sixth king of Babylon.

00:06:47.365 --> 00:06:50.118
To help solidify his control
over the people of the dynasty

00:06:50.201 --> 00:06:53.621
and eventual rule over almost
all the peoples of Mesopotamia

00:06:53.704 --> 00:06:55.998
the king used
the Code of Hammurabi,

00:06:56.082 --> 00:06:58.668
a legal text
which basically boils down

00:06:58.751 --> 00:07:00.753
to the laws of retribution.

00:07:00.837 --> 00:07:03.381
Marduk's defeat of Tiamat came
to represent the installment

00:07:03.464 --> 00:07:06.217
of the real-life Babylonian
king onto the throne

00:07:06.300 --> 00:07:08.052
as the victor over
so-called chaos.

00:07:08.136 --> 00:07:10.138
Marduk, or Bel as
he came to be known

00:07:10.221 --> 00:07:13.015
as the new national
god, provided

00:07:13.099 --> 00:07:16.310
a mythological representation
of good over evil,

00:07:16.394 --> 00:07:18.980
law over lawlessness,
a fitting metaphor

00:07:19.063 --> 00:07:20.773
for King Hammurabi's
right to rule.

00:07:20.857 --> 00:07:23.484
The myth of Tiamat and
Marduk's great battle

00:07:23.568 --> 00:07:25.778
received a ritual
portrayal every year

00:07:25.862 --> 00:07:28.573
at a spring agricultural
festival called Akitu.

00:07:28.656 --> 00:07:30.449
In the Neo-Babylonian period,

00:07:30.533 --> 00:07:32.702
this would become a
New Year celebration

00:07:32.785 --> 00:07:35.371
that lasted into the
Hellenistic period.

00:07:35.454 --> 00:07:38.916
After that, Tiamat's story
was largely lost to time.

00:07:39.000 --> 00:07:41.627
That was until the 19th century

00:07:41.711 --> 00:07:45.131
when the Enuma Elis was first
translated into English,

00:07:45.214 --> 00:07:48.134
giving Tiamat new prominence
in popular culture.

00:07:48.217 --> 00:07:50.094
Today's modern
construction of Tiamat

00:07:50.178 --> 00:07:52.013
is largely due to
Canadian author

00:07:52.096 --> 00:07:54.265
and clergyman George A. Barton,

00:07:54.348 --> 00:07:57.101
whose 1893 article
dubbed "Tiamat"

00:07:57.185 --> 00:08:00.271
compares her to the
biblical serpent Leviathan

00:08:00.354 --> 00:08:03.024
and the great red dragon
of Revelations.

00:08:03.107 --> 00:08:06.527
Most damnably, he describes
her as a female dragon,

00:08:06.611 --> 00:08:10.364
queen of a hideous host who
are hostile to the gods.

00:08:10.448 --> 00:08:13.284
He goes as far as to say
that her apocalyptic imagery

00:08:13.367 --> 00:08:14.952
indicates the popular
personification

00:08:15.036 --> 00:08:18.623
of hideousness,
arrogance, and evil.

00:08:18.706 --> 00:08:21.709
Even outside of this odd grab
bag of religious beliefs,

00:08:21.792 --> 00:08:24.712
dude clearly has it out
for the primordial goddess.

00:08:24.795 --> 00:08:28.633
Since then, Tiamat was commonly
painted as demonic chaos

00:08:28.716 --> 00:08:32.303
as other publications adopted
a negative view of her.

00:08:32.386 --> 00:08:34.388
She became, even
in her best light,

00:08:34.472 --> 00:08:36.307
the bad mother in the story.

00:08:36.390 --> 00:08:39.477
When Tiamat is adapted
for popular culture today,

00:08:39.560 --> 00:08:41.812
she retains the
evil dragon status.

00:08:41.896 --> 00:08:43.022
In "Dungeons and Dragons,"

00:08:43.105 --> 00:08:45.733
Tiamat is not a mother
but a daughter,

00:08:45.816 --> 00:08:48.861
the offspring
of the dragon creator god Io.

00:08:48.945 --> 00:08:50.988
As a five-headed dragon,
she is said to be

00:08:51.072 --> 00:08:53.658
in constant battle with
her brother, Bahamut.

00:08:53.741 --> 00:08:55.701
"Final Fantasy"
also incorporates

00:08:55.785 --> 00:08:57.411
the multi-headed dragon version

00:08:57.495 --> 00:08:59.622
into their universe as well,

00:08:59.705 --> 00:09:02.333
making Tiamat one of
the top-level bosses.

00:09:02.416 --> 00:09:04.126
Although, as the
franchise continued,

00:09:04.210 --> 00:09:05.836
she loses more and more heads.

00:09:05.920 --> 00:09:08.839
If we look back at
Tiamat's 11 monsters,

00:09:08.923 --> 00:09:13.052
it's possible to
identify precursors
to mermaids or sirens,

00:09:13.135 --> 00:09:16.055
the Minotaur, Bigfoot,
or even whatever this is.

00:09:16.138 --> 00:09:19.684
All the members of
Tiamat's monstrous brood

00:09:19.767 --> 00:09:21.185
are meant to be frightening

00:09:21.269 --> 00:09:24.522
because of their animalistic
qualities or inherent malice.

00:09:24.605 --> 00:09:26.649
Unlike her other
godly offspring,

00:09:26.732 --> 00:09:29.735
her monstrous children
were formed independently,

00:09:29.819 --> 00:09:32.280
adding another threatening,
mysterious layer

00:09:32.363 --> 00:09:33.656
to their existence.

00:09:33.739 --> 00:09:36.367
Tiamat is both
nurturer and destroyer,

00:09:36.450 --> 00:09:39.912
mother and monster,
as she chooses.

00:09:39.996 --> 00:09:43.666
After all, Tiamat and every
god and monster she birthed

00:09:43.749 --> 00:09:46.294
offers a fitting
Oedipal metaphor,

00:09:46.377 --> 00:09:49.922
fear that our creations could
someday not only overshadow us

00:09:50.006 --> 00:09:52.508
but literally destroy us.

00:09:53.676 --> 00:09:55.469
I think I said Ham.
[laughs] Let's go back.

00:09:55.553 --> 00:09:57.930
Do you think that 11 from
"Stranger Things" is 11

00:09:58.014 --> 00:09:59.598
because of this whole, like...

00:09:59.682 --> 00:10:01.934
It would be cool, right?
Eleven monsters.

00:10:02.018 --> 00:10:03.602
That's my new theory.

